,"scene_id":111254626610700,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#uk-modernism","chapters":["Sección 5.5"],"pages":[[1,132,null]]},{"id":87258312922704,"artist":"XTC","artist_id":118195318373192,"title":"Senses Working Overtime","year":null,"ess":0,"yt":"NrcemZpOmpI","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Modernismo británico: de Ultravox a U2","scene_id":111254626610700,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#uk-modernism","chapters":["Sección 5.5"],"pages":[[1,132,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":132,"text":"…trying to bridge the gap between new wave and punk-rock, notably Alternative TV. No other musicians perfected the art of the pop song as much as XTC (3). The hysterical post-industrial neurosis of White Music (1978) slowly mutated into the melodic kaleidoscope of Drums And Wires (1979), while…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":153,"text":"…The Fabulous Sounds Of The Pacific Northwest (1984), were collections of demented rock'n'roll with irresistible hooks, halfway between the Kinks and XTC. The Men Who Loved Music (1987) was still very eclectic, but focused more coherently on black music, and the sound kept evolving, becoming more…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":187,"text":"…Macabre (1987); Jazz Butcher (Pat Fish), a witty and whimsical observer; Martin Newell (who also wrote Captain Sensible's best songs), an elegant XTC-style pop composer; World Party (ex-Waterboys Karl Wallinger), an even more baroque compendium of melodic music; Peter Murphy, the former Bauhaus…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":269,"text":"…The more fashionable and rewarding route was, however, the one that coasted the baroque pop of latter-day Beach Boys, Van Dyke Parks, Big Star and XTC, the one that coupled catchy refrains and lush arrangements. The single most important school may have been San Francisco's, which had originated in…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Times Square Soundtrack Contribution (1980)","body":"XTC was included on the soundtrack for the 1980 movie *Time Square*. This soundtrack was noteworthy for being one of the initial collections to feature the nascent alternative music movement of the time, such as punk and new wave. \"The soundtrack featured the pretenders, Roxy music, Gary Newman, XTC, Joe Jackson.\"","year":1980,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Key Alt-Rock Movie Soundtracks","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zIkjd1OjCXM"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Featured on MTV","body":"XTC was one of the New Wave bands whose music videos were regularly broadcast on MTV, helping to shape the channel's early identity and popularize the genre. \"They played all the groups that we've talked about in many more. Eurythmics, XTC, Soft Sale, Adamance, Human League, Bowah Wow, and The Go Go's...\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Post-Punk Explosion Part 1: New Wave","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bYsV3ozbk4M"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Influence on Blur","body":"\"Exticy\" was mentioned as an influence on Blur's music, alongside Madchester, The Stone Roses, and New Wave, indicating the diverse range of inspirations that shaped Blur's sound. \"la demanda señó Wave incluso o demandas como Exticy.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Music Radar Clan","episode":"OASIS: Pilares del Brit Pop","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hHuqc4TgoOs"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"New Wave Powerpop","body":"XTC is listed as one of the bands whose musical output can be categorized under the new wave umbrella of Powerpop. Their style contributed to the genre's presence within the emerging alternative rock scene, characterized by clever songwriting and melodic hooks. \"We can probably include the jam, the smiths, XTC, squeeze, and the vapors.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of Power Pop","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hstJ8M2laho"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Producer Connection","body":"XTC is mentioned as one of the bands on Steve Lucky's producing resume. Lucky later worked as the producer for Radiohead's \"The Bends\" album. \"His resume also included Simple Minds, XTC and Distended Abbey Road Studios as an engineer where he worked with Pink Floyd.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Radiohead - A History: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t-G22K0vLnM"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Prolific Output and Creative Shift","body":"XTC was a prolific band, often releasing at least one album per year. In 1985, they sought a creative departure from their usual post-punk quirkiness, embracing 60s psychedelia and aiming for a more fun, less serious approach to songwriting, which led to the creation of their pseudonymous project, The Dukes of Stratosphear. \"XTC was a pretty prolific band in their day, releasing at least an album a year, but in 1985 they decided to try something different.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"If That Is Your REAL Name","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=itK5fEqnA5E"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[{"kind":"concert","title":"Time Square","body":null,"year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[]}],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":234411559887875,"type":"album","name":"White Music","list_id":223412012833492,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Zolo","src":"musthear","rank":48,"year":1978,"note":null,"yt":"nNnmPQ2284w","scaruffi":7.5},{"id":164083100392891,"type":"album","name":"Go 2","list_id":223412012833492,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Zolo","src